# ============================================================
# Every one of the 14 .NET services' docker-compose build entries uses
# `context: .` (repo root) with the shared root Dockerfile, which does
# `COPY . .` then restores/publishes just that one service's project. Without
# this file, EVERY build sends the ENTIRE monorepo as build context —
# including all other services' bin/obj (1.8GB+ across 140+ dirs after a
# session's worth of local `dotnet build` runs), every frontend/mobile app's
# node_modules (~1GB), and .git history — none of which the build actually
# needs. That multi-GB context transfer on every single build is the direct
# cause of the repeated slow builds and Docker Desktop network/daemon
# instability seen throughout this project's history (sustained heavy I/O +
# memory pressure on the Desktop VM). Keep this in sync with .gitignore's
# build-artifact patterns — this file's job is build-context size, not git
# history, so it's deliberately broader (also excludes docs/tests/deployment
# manifests no Dockerfile ever COPYs).
# ============================================================

# .NET build artifacts (every service, every project)
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/*.user

# Node — frontend (admin/storefront) and mobile (customer/staff) apps; none of
# the 14 .NET Dockerfiles ever touch these, and admin/storefront build with
# their own scoped context anyway (see their own .dockerignore).
**/node_modules/
**/dist/
**/.expo/
**/.next/

# VCS / IDE
.git/
.vs/
.vscode/
*.suo

# Not needed inside any container build
docs/
tests/
deployment/
*.md
.dockerignore
.gitignore
